body: |-
  Built in two passes (contracts, then kernel) by Kit, with the spec written in parallel by Scribe; reviewed by Aegis and Judge, one fix round, then a diff-only security re-review.

  What exists now:
  - A pure, tolerant fold and a strict write validator. Unknown kinds and fields are carried, hashed and drawn as a fallback; every such write is refused by name. A "future dimension" fixture holds that.
  - Three authorship classes: human, agent, and the unknown hand. Anything the host did not vouch for folds as unknown and never as human.
  - One clientApiId move (sha256:af070b92… to sha256:e21ffbb2…) and one modAbiId move (sha256:7aaf92f4… to sha256:fb9bd26a…); kitId unchanged. The values changed twice more inside the unmerged PR as review fixes landed, which is still one move each from the released ids.
  - The loader refuses reach 'canvas' until boards raised by offer ship.

  What review caught:
  - Aegis: an agent calling a mod command over a non-GUI connection was stamped `cli` and would have folded as a human, able to edit every node. The surface was derived from the credential and ignored the caller class. Also: a mod's own `__proto__` key survived the stamp; third-party mods received the session principal on tool calls.
  - Judge: the order-key function ran out of key space after about 385 marks drawn on top in a row, refusing the write. The main whiteboard action. The existing test used random positions and never reached it.
  - The test Judge asked for on superseded pins found a real hole: a superseded first-party version could be freshly installed at first-party tier.
  - Scribe: a builder commit had find-and-replaced an id literal inside a history row of the spec, rewriting what an earlier move recorded.

  The upgrade problem, found by asking "what happens on a user's machine": the three first-party mods hard-code the ABI id, so after upgrade the host would refuse them and nothing would reinstall them; the founder would have typed a password three times, behind a misleading DOWN message. Arky proposed five options. Aegis approved the narrowest: the host accepts the one prior id for a snapshot whose exact package hash is listed in the signed kernel, first-party by hash, package source, declaring no tools. It grants nothing, and a test fails at the next ABI move until the list is emptied. Verified first that the new mod trees differ from the old only in a version line and the id constant.

  Process: a builder reported a suite green after running it without its bundle check, and said so himself in the next report. Reviewers again worked without a shell; every finding the builder checked reproduced.
